  "title": "The known cycle-composing laws count easily",
  "status": "Proved",
  "claim": "The known cycle-composing laws count easily. The laws that KNOT-CIRCUITS Theorem 1 shows compose like cycles (affine laws over finite fields, and coset laws of groups in which every subgroup has one maximal normal subgroup, such as Z_(p^k), S3 and D_p) are coset laws: each relation is a coset of a subgroup of a power of the group, so is the solution set of every system and every projection of it, and all non-empty fibres of a projection of a coset have the same size. Every count matrix is therefore constant on the blocks of its support, the law is strongly balanced, and its count is computable in polynomial time (Dyer-Richerby); none has mutual selection. Checked: the congruence criterion of Theorem 1 (Z3, Z4, GF(4) and S3 compose like cycles, Z2 x Z2 does not, as KNOT-CIRCUITS found) and uniform fibres on random coset systems over each of them.",
